# `git hop repair` is its own command, not an alias for `git hop doctor`.
#
# Ported from TestRepair_DoctorAliasGone in the Go e2e suite.
#
# The two commands were once the same thing, and the failure mode this
# guards is a regression back to that: repair silently re-acquiring doctor's
# help text, its flags, or its behaviour. Help text is the cheapest place to
# catch it, because an alias inherits the parent's Short/Long verbatim.
#
# Asserting only "repair --help mentions stale worktrees" would not catch
# it -- an alias could grow that sentence too. The assertion that matters is
# the pair: repair describes ITSELF and does NOT describe doctor, while
# doctor still describes doctor. If repair were an alias, both would print
# the same text and the negative assertion would fail.
#
# No repository is needed: --help is answered by the command tree before any
# hub lookup happens.

exec git-hop repair --help
stdout 'Repair stale worktree metadata'
stdout 'gitdir pointers, hop\.json, git registry'

# repair's own flags, which doctor does not have.
stdout '--list-backups'
stdout '--undo'
stdout '-n, --dry-run'

# doctor's Short must not appear here.
! stdout 'Check and repair the environment'

# --- doctor is still its own command, and still says its own thing ---
# This half is what turns the negative assertion above into evidence: the
# doctor string exists in the binary, it is simply attached to the other
# command. Without it, deleting doctor entirely would also satisfy the
# negative assertion.
#
# Each command's Short surfaces in the root command list, so the two rows
# there are the cleanest side-by-side proof that these are two commands
# with two descriptions rather than one command under two names.
exec git-hop --help
stdout 'doctor\s+Check and repair the environment'
stdout 'repair\s+Safely repair stale worktree metadata'

exec git-hop doctor --help
stdout 'Run diagnostics on git-hop installation and project setup'
! stdout 'Repair stale worktree metadata'
